  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Why English-style names sound real",
      "content": "<p>Real English place names are made of a few dozen recurring parts, and readers know them without realising. A name ending in <em>-thorpe</em>, <em>-by</em> or <em>-wick</em> sounds like a village because thousands of real villages end that way. Add a second word the way real places do, such as a church (St Giles), a position (Lower, Nether, Great), a river (-on-the-Wold) or a market (Chipping, Market), and the name gains a history.</p><p>Our Naming Library page on English place-name elements explains what each part originally meant, so you can build names that describe the place: a <em>-combe</em> sits in a valley, a <em>-hithe</em> is a landing place on a river or coast.</p>"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Naming a whole map",
      "content": "<p>Places in the same region should sound related. Pick two or three endings for each region and reuse them, as real counties do: a coast full of <em>-hithe</em> and <em>-cove</em> towns, a north of <em>-gard</em> and <em>-heim</em>. Then let the odd name break the pattern to show history, like an elven ruin in human lands or a dwarven name on a hill fort.</p><p>Vary the length too. A map where every town has four syllables is hard to read; mix short names like Ashscar with longer ones like Dunbourne-on-the-Wold.</p>"
    }
  ],
  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "How do you name a fantasy town?",
      "answer": "Decide what the town is known for (fishing, mining, a market, a castle) and who lives there, then build the name from a feature and a place-name ending: Gull + port, Anvil + fell. Adding a second word, as in Chipping Saltford or Wynthorpe St Margaret, makes it sound older."
    },
    {
      "question": "Do fantasy town names have meanings?",
      "answer": "Many of the English-style ones do, because they are built from real Old English place-name parts: -thorpe was an outlying farm, -combe a valley and -wick a farm or trading place. The elven and dwarven names are invented and have no meaning in a real language."
    },
    {
      "question": "How do you name a medieval town?",
      "answer": "Use English place-name patterns: a personal name or feature plus an ending such as -by, -thorpe, -ham or -ton, and perhaps a church or market word, like Market Mercerwich. Our village generator's medieval style builds names from Old English and Old Norse personal names."
    }
  ],
